<question type="stack">
  <name><text>Ch09_P04_Structure_of_the_proof</text></name>
  <questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>Here is the proof of the example above in numbered lines.</p>
<table>
<tr><td>1</td><td>Suppose that \(r\) is a nonzero rational number.</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>Then \(r = \dfrac{a}{b}\) for some integers \(a\) and \(b\) with \(b \neq 0\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>We can write \(r = \sqrt{2} \times \dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}}\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>We know that \(\sqrt{2}\) is irrational.</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>Suppose, for contradiction, that \(\dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}}\) is rational.</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Then \(\dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}} = \dfrac{c}{d}\) for some integers \(c\) and \(d\) with \(d \neq 0\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>So \(\sqrt{2} = r \times \dfrac{d}{c} = \dfrac{a}{b} \times \dfrac{d}{c} = \dfrac{ad}{bc}\).</td></tr>
<tr><td>8</td><td>Since \(ad\) and \(bc\) are integers, \(\sqrt{2}\) is rational. This contradicts line 4.</td></tr>
<tr><td>9</td><td>So \(\dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}}\) is irrational.</td></tr>
<tr><td>10</td><td>So \(r = \sqrt{2} \times \dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}}\) is a product of two irrational numbers.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>(a) The proof contains a proof by contradiction inside a direct proof. In which line does the inner proof by contradiction begin?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]] [[feedback:prt1]]</div>
<p>(b) How do the two parts fit together?</p>
<div>[[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]] [[feedback:prt2]]</div>
<p>(c) Line 7 divides by \(c\). Which earlier line guarantees that \(c \neq 0\)?</p>
<div>Line [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]] [[feedback:prt3]]</div>
<p>(d) Would the statement still be true without the word ‘nonzero’?</p>
<div>[[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]] [[feedback:prt4]]</div>]]></text></questiontext>
  <generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<p>The proof has two modules. The outer one is direct: it starts from a nonzero rational \(r\) (lines 1 and 2), writes \(r = \sqrt{2} \times \dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}}\) (line 3), and needs both factors to be irrational. The first factor is irrational by a known result (line 4). The second needs a proof of its own, a proof by contradiction (lines 5 to 9): assume \(\dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}} = \dfrac{c}{d}\), deduce that \(\sqrt{2}\) is rational, and contradict line 4. Line 10 puts the two together.</p>
<p>Line 7 divides by \(c\). We know \(c \neq 0\) because \(r \neq 0\) (line 1), so \(\dfrac{r}{\sqrt{2}} \neq 0\), and so \(\dfrac{c}{d} \neq 0\). This is where ‘nonzero’ is used, and it is needed: 0 is not a product of two irrational numbers, since \(xy = 0\) forces \(x = 0\) or \(y = 0\), and 0 is rational. (A proof that breaks does not on its own show that a statement is false; here the statement is false for 0 for this separate reason.)</p>]]></text></generalfeedback>
